<h1>Projects</h1>

<p> In addition to our reenactment activities, we have a few
  longer-term research projects into various aspects of Roman life.
  We collaborate with other reenactors and groups, academic
  archeologists, heritage trades and crafts people, and others to
  explore topics of interest.


<h2 class="project">Panis Quadratus</h2>

<img class="project-left-image" src="images/panis-quadratus-pompeii.jpg">

<p><i>Panis quadratus</i> is a Roman bread, baked as a circular load,
with eight divisions marked on the top for easy division.  We have
sculptures, murals, and actual carbonised loaves from Pompeii showing
this bread, and recipes from multiple sources.  However, the recipes
aren't as precise as they are today, and determining the exact
ingredients and quantities to be used is difficult.

<p>This is a long-term project, doing ongoing experimentation with
different grains, quantities, and cooking methods.  This is a popular
experimental archeology topic, with many others interested across the
world from both academia and the enthusiast communities.


<h2 class="project">Artillery<h2>

<h2 class="project">Pilum Rivets</h2>

<img class="project-left-image" src="images/pilum-shield-1.jpg">

<p>The Roman <i>pilum</i> is a heavy javelin, with a small
armour-piercing head, a long iron shank, and a heavy wooden shaft.
The design evolved over its period of use, but in one design, the
wooden shaft and the long iron shank were joined by rivets.

<p>In Plutach's <i>The Life of Marius</i> is a quote:
<div class="quote">
And it is said that it was in preparation for this battle that
Marius introduced an innovation in the structure of the javelin. Up to
this time, it seems, that part of the shaft which was let into the
iron head was fastened there by two iron nails; but now, leaving one
of these as it was, Marius removed the other, and put in its place a
wooden pin that could easily be broken. His design was that the
javelin, after striking the enemy's shield, should not stand straight
out, but that the wooden peg should break, thus allowing the shaft to
bend in the iron head and trail along the ground, being held fast by
the twist at the point of the weapon.
</div>

<p>Despite becoming a popular "fact" about Roman pila, whether this
"innovation" actually occured has long been debated by historians and
archeologists.  This project aims to investigate whether it would
actually work by experimentation with various wooden pins, using pila
\against both Roman and barbarian shields, and contribute our results
to the debate.

<h2 class="project">Roman Forging and Metalworking</h2>



